              "en": "# China · K1 · Lesson 1\n## China Colours and National Day\n\n## 1. Lesson Identity\n\n- Country / Place: China\n- Grade: K1\n- Lesson: 1 of 4\n\n## 2. Lesson Focus\n\nChildren meet China through red, gold, stars, flags, and National Day decorations. The focus is visual, sensory, and celebratory rather than informational.\n\n## 3. Learning Objectives\n\nChildren will:\n\n- notice red, gold, and star shapes as part of the China celebration environment;\n- point to, match, place, or arrange simple China-related visual symbols;\n- participate in decorating a small China corner or celebration space and experience China as a familiar class celebration.\n\n## 4. Teacher Language\n\nTeacher Input:\n\n- Look. Red and gold.\n- I see a star.\n- This is China.\n- This is a flag.\n- Let’s decorate.\n- Put the star here.\n- Do you like red?\n- Wave the flag.\n- Happy National Day.\n\nPossible Child Response:\n\n- red\n- gold\n- star\n- flag\n- China\n- here\n- I see a star.\n- I like red.\n\nChildren may also respond by pointing, waving, choosing, placing, or matching. Full verbal output should not be required.\n\n## 5. Suggested Games and Activities\n\n### Activity 1: Red and Gold Treasure Hunt\n\n**Purpose:**  \nHelp children notice the main visual colours of the China celebration environment.\n\n**Materials:**  \nRed and gold paper, fabric pieces, cards, ribbons, stars, baskets or trays.\n\n**Preparation:**  \nPlace red and gold items around the classroom at child height. Prepare two baskets or trays: one for red items and one for gold items.\n\n**How to Play / Do:**  \nInvite children to walk around the classroom and find red or gold objects. Children pick up one item at a time and place it in the matching basket. The teacher repeats simple language such as “red,” “gold,” “I see red,” and “Put it here.”\n\nAfter collecting, children sit together and look at the baskets. The teacher briefly reviews the colours and connects them to the China celebration corner.\n\n**Teacher Notes:**  \nKeep the pace slow and concrete. For children who are not ready to name colours, accepting pointing, holding, or placing is enough.\n\n### Activity 2: Star Match and Place\n\n**Purpose:**  \nHelp children recognise stars as a repeated visual element in the China theme.\n\n**Materials:**  \nLarge star cut-outs, small star stickers, red background paper, trays, glue sticks or removable adhesive.\n\n**Preparation:**  \nPrepare a large red background sheet for the class. Place star shapes in a basket. If using glue, pre-mark simple placement areas to reduce waiting time.\n\n**How to Play / Do:**  \nShow children one large star and say, “I see a star.” Invite each child to choose a star and place it on the red background. Children can match big and small stars, count a few stars if appropriate, or simply place one star on the display.\n\nThe finished red-and-star paper can become the first piece of the China celebration corner.\n\n**Teacher Notes:**  \nDo not explain national symbolism in detail. The aim is visual recognition and participation, not symbolic memorisation.\n\n### Activity 3: Flag Wave and Celebration Walk\n\n**Purpose:**  \nUse movement to make the China celebration theme active and memorable.\n\n**Materials:**  \nSmall safe paper flags or red-gold ribbons, simple music if available, open classroom or theatre space.\n\n**Preparation:**  \nPrepare enough small flags or ribbons for children. Clear a simple walking path. Avoid sticks or sharp flag holders for K1.\n\n**How to Play / Do:**  \nGive each child a small flag or ribbon. Model slow waving, high waving, low waving, and walking together. Use repeated language: “Wave the flag,” “Walk,” “Stop,” “Red,” “Gold,” and “Happy National Day.”\n\nAfter the movement, children return their flags or ribbons to the China corner and help arrange them in a basket or display container.\n\n**Teacher Notes:**  \nKeep movement safe and controlled. The activity should feel like a gentle class celebration, not a performance drill.",
              "zh": "# 中国 · K1 · 第 1 课\n## 中国色彩与国庆节\n\n## 1. 课程身份\n\n- 国家 / 地区：中国\n- 年级：K1\n- 课次：4 课中的第 1 课\n\n## 2. 课程重点\n\n幼儿通过红色、金色、星星、国旗和国庆节装饰认识中国。重点是视觉、感官和庆祝感，而不是知识讲解。\n\n## 3. 学习目标\n\n幼儿将：\n\n- 注意红、金色和星星形状，作为中国节日环境的一部分；\n- 用指、匹配、摆放或排列的方式回应简单的中国相关视觉符号；\n- 参与到装饰小中国角或庆祝空间的过程中，把中国当作一次熟悉的班级庆祝。\n\n## 4. 教师语言\n\n教师输入：\n\n- Look. Red and gold.\n- I see a star.\n- This is China.\n- This is a flag.\n- Let’s decorate.\n- Put the star here.\n- Do you like red?\n- Wave the flag.\n- Happy National Day.\n\n幼儿可能回应：\n\n- red\n- gold\n- star\n- flag\n- China\n- here\n- I see a star.\n- I like red.\n\n幼儿也可以用指、挥动、选择、摆放或匹配来回应。不要求完整地说出整句英文。\n\n## 5. 建议游戏与活动\n\n### 活动 1：红色和金色寻宝\n\n**目的：**\n帮助幼儿注意中国节日环境里最主要的视觉色彩。\n\n**材料：**\n红色和金色的纸、布片、卡片、丝带、星星、篮子或托盘。\n\n**准备：**\n把红色和金色物品分散放在教室里幼儿够得到的高度。准备两个篮子或托盘：一个放红色物品，一个放金色物品。\n\n**玩法 / 做法：**\n邀请幼儿在教室里走动，寻找红色或金色的物品。幼儿一次拿一件物品，放进对应颜色的篮子里。教师重复简单的语言，如\"red\"\"gold\"\"I see red\"\"Put it here\"。\n\n收完以后，幼儿围坐一起看篮子。教师简单回顾颜色，并把这些颜色和中国庆祝角连起来。\n\n**教师提示：**\n节奏要慢、具体。对还不会说出颜色名称的幼儿，能指、能拿、能摆放就已经足够。\n\n### 活动 2：星星匹配与摆放\n\n**目的：**\n帮助幼儿认识\"星星\"是这次中国主题里反复出现的视觉元素。\n\n**材料：**\n大星星剪纸、小星星贴纸、红色背景纸、托盘、胶棒或可移除粘贴。\n\n**准备：**\n准备一张大的红色背景纸。把星星放在一个篮子里。如果用胶水，提前在背景上标好大致位置，避免幼儿等待。\n\n**玩法 / 做法：**\n教师先出示一颗大星星，说\"I see a star\"。再请每位幼儿选一颗星星，把它放在红色背景上。幼儿可以匹配大星星和小星星，如果合适也可以数一数几颗星星，或者就只放一颗到展示上。\n\n完成后，这张\"红色和星星\"作品可以成为中国庆祝角的第一件布置。\n\n**教师提示：**\n不要详细讲解国旗的象征意义。重点是视觉辨认和参与，不是符号记忆。\n\n### 活动 3：挥动国旗和庆祝走\n\n**目的：**\n通过动作让中国庆祝主题变得更生动、印象更深。\n\n**材料：**\n安全的小纸旗或红金色丝带；如有简单音乐也可以准备；教室或活动区留出可走动的空间。\n\n**准备：**\n为每位幼儿准备一面小旗或一条丝带。清理出一条简单的行走路线。K1 不要使用带尖头或硬杆的旗杆。\n\n**玩法 / 做法：**\n给每位幼儿一面小旗或一条丝带。教师示范慢慢挥、举高挥、压低挥以及一起走的动作。反复使用语言：\"Wave the flag\"\"Walk\"\"Stop\"\"Red\"\"Gold\"\"Happy National Day\"。\n\n走完以后，幼儿把小旗或丝带送回中国角，一起把它们收到篮子里或展示容器里。\n\n**教师提示：**\n动作要安全、可控。整体感受应该是一次温柔的班级庆祝，而不是表演式操练。"
