            "markdown": {
              "en": "# China · K3 · Lesson 1\n## Chinese Characters and Writing\n\n## 1. Lesson Identity\n\n- Country / Place: China\n- Grade: K3\n- Lesson: 1 of 4\n\n## 2. Lesson Focus\n\nChildren explore Chinese characters as a visual writing system. They notice strokes, shapes, simple picture-character connections, and try character-inspired mark-making or rubbing.\n\n## 3. Learning Objectives\n\nChildren will:\n\n- notice that Chinese characters are made from strokes and shapes;\n- connect some simple characters with pictures or meanings;\n- use brush-style marks, rubbing, tracing, or matching to explore writing visually and explain one simple idea about writing.\n\n## 4. Teacher Language\n\nTeacher Input:\n\n- Look at this Chinese character.\n- What can you see?\n- It has lines and strokes.\n- This character means mountain / sun / tree.\n- Let’s trace the strokes.\n- Try it slowly.\n- This is writing.\n- We can write a message.\n- Which character do you choose?\n- Put your character on the wall.\n\nPossible Child Response:\n\n- I see lines.\n- It looks like a tree.\n- This is sun / mountain / tree.\n- I choose this one.\n- I can write it.\n- This is my character.\n- It is difficult / easy.\n\n## 5. Suggested Games and Activities\n\n### Activity 1: Picture to Character Match\n\n**Purpose:**  \nHelp children notice that some characters can be connected to simple pictures or meanings.\n\n**Materials:**  \nPicture cards and matching simple character cards, such as sun, moon, mountain, tree, person, mouth, hand, or water. Use only a small set.\n\n**Preparation:**  \nPrepare large cards with clear visuals. Place picture cards on one side and character cards on the other. Avoid using too many characters at once.\n\n**How to Play / Do:**  \nShow one picture, such as a mountain. Ask children what they see. Then show the character and invite them to notice shapes or lines. Children work in pairs or small groups to match picture cards with character cards.\n\n**Teacher Notes:**  \nThis is not formal literacy instruction. The goal is visual noticing and meaning connection, not accurate memorisation or writing test performance.\n\n### Activity 2: Big Brush Stroke Lab\n\n**Purpose:**  \nAllow children to experience writing as movement, control, and visual creation.\n\n**Materials:**  \nLarge paper, water brushes, thick brushes, black paint or diluted ink substitute, trays, aprons, wipes, and sample stroke cards.\n\n**Preparation:**  \nSet up a protected table or floor area. Prepare a few simple stroke cards: horizontal line, vertical line, dot, turn, and sweep.\n\n**How to Play / Do:**  \nChildren practise making large strokes on paper. The teacher models slow, controlled movements. Children then choose one simple character or character-like composition to try. They can work individually or contribute to a shared “Chinese writing wall.”\n\n**Teacher Notes:**  \nAccept age-appropriate attempts. Focus on direction, control, pressure, and visual expression. Do not correct children as if this were formal calligraphy training.\n\n### Activity 3: Character Museum Wall\n\n**Purpose:**  \nHelp children organise and present their writing exploration.\n\n**Materials:**  \nChildren’s brush papers, picture-character pairs, sticky labels, display board or wall space, tape, and name cards.\n\n**Preparation:**  \nCreate sections on the wall, such as “Pictures,” “Characters,” and “Our Writing.” Prepare simple labels if useful.\n\n**How to Play / Do:**  \nChildren choose one picture-character pair or one writing piece to add to the wall. They place their work and tell a peer or teacher what they chose. Stronger children may explain the connection between picture and character.\n\n**Teacher Notes:**  \nEncourage children to speak in short, clear phrases. Allow pointing and one-word answers for children who are less confident, but invite K3 children to explain when ready.",
              "zh": "# 中国 · K3 · 第 1 课\n## 汉字与书写\n\n## 1. 课程身份\n\n- 国家 / 地区：中国\n- 年级：K3\n- 课次：4 课中的第 1 课\n\n## 2. 课程重点\n\n幼儿把汉字作为一种\"可视的书写系统\"来探索。他们会注意笔画、形状、简单的\"像图画\"的联系，并尝试毛笔式的笔触、拓印、描摹或匹配。\n\n## 3. 学习目标\n\n幼儿将：\n\n- 注意到汉字是由笔画和形状组成的；\n- 把一些简单的汉字和对应的图或意思连起来；\n- 通过毛笔式笔触、拓印、描摹或匹配的方式在视觉上探索书写，并能就\"什么是书写\"说出一句简单的看法。\n\n## 4. 教师语言\n\n教师输入：\n\n- Look at this Chinese character.\n- What can you see?\n- It has lines and strokes.\n- This character means mountain / sun / tree.\n- Let’s trace the strokes.\n- Try it slowly.\n- This is writing.\n- We can write a message.\n- Which character do you choose?\n- Put your character on the wall.\n\n幼儿可能回应：\n\n- I see lines.\n- It looks like a tree.\n- This is sun / mountain / tree.\n- I choose this one.\n- I can write it.\n- This is my character.\n- It is difficult / easy.\n\n## 5. 建议游戏与活动\n\n### 活动 1：图与汉字配对\n\n**目的：**\n帮助幼儿注意到：一些汉字可以和简单的图、意思连起来。\n\n**材料：**\n图片卡和对应的简单汉字卡，比如：日、月、山、树、人、口、手或水。一次只用少量字。\n\n**准备：**\n准备清晰、图大字大的卡片。图片卡放一边，汉字卡放另一边。一次不要放太多字。\n\n**玩法 / 做法：**\n教师先出示一张图，比如\"山\"。请幼儿说一说看到了什么。再出示汉字，请幼儿注意形状和线条。幼儿两人一组或几人一组，把图片和汉字配对。\n\n**教师提示：**\n这不是正式的识字课。目标是\"看见\"和\"建立意义联系\"，不是准确记忆，也不是写字测验。\n\n### 活动 2：大毛笔笔触实验室\n\n**目的：**\n让幼儿把\"写字\"体验为一种动作、控制和视觉创造。\n\n**材料：**\n大纸、水笔、粗毛笔、黑色颜料或稀释墨水替代品、托盘、围裙、抹布，以及笔画示例卡。\n\n**准备：**\n布置好防水的桌面或地面区。准备几种简单笔画的卡：横、竖、点、折、撇。\n\n**玩法 / 做法：**\n幼儿在大纸上练这些大笔触。教师示范慢而稳的动作。幼儿之后可以选一个简单汉字或\"像汉字\"的组合来试一试。可以独立做，也可以一起往一面\"中国字墙\"上贴。\n\n**教师提示：**\n接受 K3 水平的尝试。重点是方向、控制、力度和视觉表达。不要像教正式书法那样\"纠正\"孩子。\n\n### 活动 3：汉字博物馆墙\n\n**目的：**\n帮助幼儿把\"书写探索\"整理并展示出来。\n\n**材料：**\n幼儿的毛笔作品、图字配对卡、可写小标签、展示板或墙面、胶带、名字卡。\n\n**准备：**\n在墙上分出几个区，比如\"图\"\"字\"\"我们的字\"。如需要可以准备简单标签。\n\n**玩法 / 做法：**\n幼儿选一组\"图字配对\"或一件\"写字作品\"贴到墙上。贴好以后，向同伴或教师说一说自己选的是哪个。能力较强的幼儿可以解释图和字之间的联系。\n\n**教师提示：**\n鼓励幼儿用简短清晰的句子说。表达不够自信的幼儿可以指一指或说一个词；K3 幼儿如果准备好了，可以邀请他们多说一句。"
            }
