        "overview": {
          "en": "# Kenya · K1 Overview\n\n## 1. Country Character\n\nKenya is a vivid and highly visual country theme for young children. It can be introduced through open grasslands, large animals, warm colours, simple movement, drums, patterns, and outdoor exploration. For K1 children, Kenya should not be presented as a fact-heavy geography unit. It works best as an encounter with big animals, wide spaces, animal movement, natural colours, and simple shared making.\n\n## 2. Grade-Level Lens\n\nFor K1, Kenya is approached through sensory experience, visual recognition, movement, and pretend play. Children look at large animal pictures, move like animals, touch textured materials, build simple grassland scenes, and create bold pattern work. The main lens is: “What animals can we see? How do they move? What colours and patterns can we notice?”\n\n## 3. Classroom Setup and Key Elements\n\nThe classroom can include a Kenya discovery corner with grassland images, large animal cards, brown-green-yellow fabric, animal footprints, simple drums or shakers, pattern strips, baskets, blocks, and small-world animals. The setup should feel open and natural. Large pictures and concrete props are more useful than maps or written information.\n\n## 4. Four-Lesson Map\n\n### Lesson 1: Meet Kenya — Grassland and Big Animals\n\nChildren enter the Kenya theme through wide grassland images and large animals such as giraffes, zebras, elephants, and lions. They notice size, colour, movement, and simple animal features.\n\n### Lesson 2: Animal Moves and Footprints\n\nChildren explore how animals move and what footprints they may leave. They crawl, stomp, stretch, tiptoe, and match simple footprint shapes to animal pictures.\n\n### Lesson 3: Colours, Patterns and Textures\n\nChildren look closely at zebra stripes, giraffe spots, animal fur textures, grassland colours, and simple pattern materials. They make a bold Kenya-inspired pattern collage.\n\n### Lesson 4: Build a Mini Kenya Grassland\n\nChildren use blocks, fabric, paper grass, animals, and their own artwork to build a shared mini grassland and show where animals can stand, move, hide, or rest.",
          "zh": "# 肯尼亚 · K1 概览\n\n## 1. 国家特点\n\n肯尼亚是一个色彩鲜明、视觉感很强的国家主题，特别适合幼儿。可以从开阔的草原、大型动物、暖色、简单的动作、鼓点、图案和户外探索的角度来介绍。对 K1 幼儿来说，肯尼亚不应被讲成一份厚厚的地理知识课。更好的方式是一次\"遇见大型动物、感受开阔空间、模仿动物动作、注意自然颜色、共同动手做\"的体验。\n\n## 2. 年级视角\n\n对 K1 来说，应当通过感官体验、视觉辨认、动作游戏和假装游戏来认识肯尼亚。幼儿看大型动物图片、模仿动物动作、触摸有质感的材料、搭建简单的草原场景，并做出大色块图案作品。核心视角是：**\"我们能看到哪些动物？它们怎么动？我们可以注意到哪些颜色和图案？\"**\n\n## 3. 教室布置与关键元素\n\n教室可以布置一个\"肯尼亚发现角\"：草原图片、大型动物卡、棕绿黄色布料、动物脚印、简单的鼓或沙锤、图案条、篮子、积木和小世界动物。布置应当让人感到开阔、自然。大幅图片和具体的道具比地图或文字信息更有用。\n\n## 4. 四课地图\n\n### 第 1 课：认识肯尼亚——草原和大型动物\n\n幼儿通过开阔的草原图片和大型动物进入肯尼亚主题——长颈鹿、斑马、大象、狮子。他们注意大小、颜色、动作和简单的动物特征。\n\n### 第 2 课：动物的动和脚印\n\n幼儿探索动物怎么动、可能留下什么样的脚印。他们爬、踏、伸长、踮脚，并把简单的脚印形状和动物图片匹配起来。\n\n### 第 3 课：颜色、图案和触感\n\n幼儿仔细看斑马条纹、长颈鹿斑点、动物皮毛的质感、草原的颜色，以及简单的图案材料。他们做出一幅受肯尼亚启发的大色块拼贴。\n\n### 第 4 课：搭建一座迷你肯尼亚草原\n\n幼儿用积木、布、纸草、动物和之前做的作品，共同搭出一片小草原，展示动物可以站立、移动、躲藏或休息的地方。"
        },
